<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Block vs. Inline Elements</title>
    <style>
        /* Adding backgrounds and borders so you can visually see the boundaries */
        .block-demo {
            background-color: #d1e7dd;
            border: 2px solid #0f5132;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            padding: 8px;
        }

        .inline-demo {
            background-color: #fff3cd;
            border: 2px solid #664d03;
            padding: 2px 4px;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>

    <!-- 1. BLOCK-LEVEL ELEMENTS -->
    <h2>1. Block-Level Elements</h2>
    <p>Block elements always start on a new line and stretch across the full available width.</p>

    <div class="block-demo">This is a &lt;div&gt; (Block element)</div>
    <p class="block-demo">This is a &lt;p&gt; (Block element) — notice how it drops to a new line below the div.</p>
    <h3 class="block-demo">This is an &lt;h3&gt; (Block element)</h3>

    <hr>

    <!-- 2. INLINE ELEMENTS -->
    <h2>2. Inline Elements</h2>
    <p>Inline elements sit side-by-side on the same line and only take up as much width as their content needs.</p>

    <span class="inline-demo">First &lt;span&gt;</span>
    <strong class="inline-demo">Second &lt;strong&gt;</strong>
    <em class="inline-demo">Third &lt;em&gt;</em>
    <a href="#" class="inline-demo">Fourth &lt;a&gt; link</a>

    <hr>

    <!-- 3. MIXING BLOCK AND INLINE ELEMENTS -->
    <h2>3. Nesting Inline Inside Block</h2>
    <div class="block-demo">
        This entire green box is a block-level <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>. Inside it, you can place inline elements like 
        <span class="inline-demo">a &lt;span&gt;</span> or <mark class="inline-demo">a &lt;mark&gt;</mark> 
        without breaking the flow of the sentence.
    </div>

</body>

</html>